{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_005 abstracts SRC_002 by recasting the latter's task-space partition (checklist-covered vs. boundary-violating) as a special case of an adversarial perturbation: the evaluation design is REFUTED IF SRC_002's stratification performance gap between compliance and competence does not vanish on non-adversarial, in-distribution items where the checklist's mapping is clean, contradicting SRC_005's requirement that the distinction is observable only under distribution shift.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"42e7239f4c740c62fc37cbefb2d141a1514e5ed7fea7638bf2c839969bbd5912"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3f62d2700409e96561ede442a6db7f83270536be3b303689beebee24cc051dea"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_004 and SRC_001 contradict each other on the role of checklist adherence because SRC_004 uses dissociation from the checklist on expert-strength items as the signal of competence, while SRC_001 assumes the panel relies on the checklist as a constraint and tests for genuine gain via error-set partitioning. This contradiction is REFUTED IF a third system that uses the checklist as a non-binding prompt (neither strict constraint nor fully dissociated) dominates both, demonstrating that the strict partition between compliance and competence is an experimental artifact.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"aa7d19fc4214daeade50500bba29f0daa172cfaccc1dd70084437d6c886d4505"},{"role":"dependence","target":"6869b429a7bc598811bbd22689ca838ea16024d4dcc243c2a701ef9ec3f90b44"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"SRC_003 reduces to SRC_002 because SRC_003's distinction between a compliant and genuinely reliable panel—measured by divergence on misleading items—is mechanistically identical to SRC_002's partition of items by the checklist's action-coverage. SRC_003's 'misleading items' are precisely SRC_002's 'boundary-violating items'. This reduction is REFUTED IF SRC_003's misleading items are defined by a contextual